{
  "bytes": 3044,
  "n_tools": 1,
  "request": {
    "model": "openrouter/free",
    "messages": [
      {
        "role": "system",
        "content": "You are construct's learning pass. You read a finished stretch of an agent session and improve two durable stores so the next session goes better. You can only use the memory, skill and skill_manage tools. Do the work in as few calls as you can, then reply with one short line saying what changed (or 'Nothing to save.')."
      },
      {
        "role": "user",
        "content": "<session>\nUSER: release the crate. I always do this on Fridays.\n\n\nTOOL CALL shell: {\"command\":\"ls /nonexistent-release-dir\"}\n\nTOOL RESULT: Error: ls: cannot access '/nonexistent-release-dir': No such file or directory\n\n[exit 2]\n\n\nTOOL CALL shell: {\"command\":\"exit 2\"}\n\nTOOL RESULT: Error: (no output)\n[exit 2]\n\n\nTOOL CALL shell: {\"command\":\"false\"}\n\nTOOL RESULT: Error: (no output)\n[exit 1]\n\nASSISTANT: released\n</session>\n\nCurrent memory: (empty)\n\nSkill library: empty.\n\nMEMORY — who the user is and what their machine is like.\n- Save only what the user said about how they want you to work (\"I prefer…\", \"always…\", \"don't…\", a correction of your approach), facts about them, and stable facts about the machine (OS quirks, toolchains, paths, services).\n- A task request is not a preference. \"Document X in the README\" or \"fix it in the library, not the tests\" describe one task; never record them as standing preferences or habits.\n- Use target 'user' for the person, 'memory' for the machine and its tools in general, and 'project' for how the current repo works (build and test steps, landmines, conventions): project notes only show up in this repo. All are capped: batch your changes in one call and remove or merge stale entries to make room.\n- Skip task progress, one-off details, anything easy to rediscover, and secrets.\n\nIf nothing in the session meets the bar, reply 'Nothing to save.' and stop. A routine session that went smoothly usually has nothing to save.\n"
      }
    ],
    "stream": true,
    "stream_options": {
      "include_usage": true
    },
    "tools": [
      {
        "type": "function",
        "function": {
          "name": "memory",
          "description": "Durable memory injected into future sessions. target 'user' = who the user is and how they like to work; 'memory' = facts about this machine and its tools; 'project' = how the current repo works (build/test steps, landmines, conventions), shown only when working in this repo. Both are capped: pass all changes as one operations array (applied atomically, cap checked on the result), removing stale entries to make room. Save preferences, corrections and stable facts; skip task progress and things easy to rediscover.",
          "parameters": {
            "type": "object",
            "properties": {
              "target": {
                "type": "string",
                "enum": [
                  "user",
                  "memory",
                  "project"
                ]
              },
              "operations": {
                "type": "array",
                "items": {
                  "type": "object",
                  "properties": {
                    "action": {
                      "type": "string",
                      "enum": [
                        "add",
                        "replace",
                        "remove"
                      ]
                    },
                    "content": {
                      "type": "string"
                    },
                    "old_text": {
                      "type": "string",
                      "description": "Unique substring of the entry to replace/remove"
                    }
                  },
                  "required": [
                    "action"
                  ]
                }
              }
            },
            "required": [
              "target",
              "operations"
            ]
          }
        }
      }
    ],
    "max_tokens": 4000
  }
